recommendations.text:
Delay Tactics: none. This is the last round in which they could be ordered, so the Board states its view plainly:
- bottleneck and both are never acceptable;
- poaching fails the peak-cash line in 2035-2036 and would be vetoed;
- the team's stricter condition should stay standing, as the CEO kept it in 2030;
- nothing on Delay Tactics in any public line.
The supply-security other_move (in-house work packages, titanium, rare earths) must stay confined to Airbus's own ramp. The Ethics, Compliance and Sustainability Committee should continue to be informed.

The CFO's papers, carried over from the 2030 review:
- a year-by-year net-cash bridge for 2035-2037 that includes the dividend, on the FY2025 base and on the run-rate;
- confirmation of the payout guardrail (lower end of 30-50% if ramp cash falls short);
- the effect of the single $35.52B charge at the 2037 EIS on reported earnings and on the payout base, with no dividend cut and no equity raise outside a crisis;
- the break-evens, reported if crossed: an NGSA bill above $36.4B, FCF below EUR 4.61bn, or a rate below $1.068 per EUR.
Refundable advances, state support and risk-sharing remain upside and are never assumed in the case.

Operating head: confirm the following before entry into service:
- NGSA production readiness for 2037;
- the A320neo ramp shield, with no supply crunch;
- engines and critical materials secured for both families through the transition.
The Board also asks for his safety and quality results, with any five-pillars flag.

Public statement:
- give windows, not dates: NGSA on track for entry into service in the second half of the 2030s;
- put safety first, with a disciplined and controlled ramp;
- nothing timed to Boeing and nothing on Delay Tactics.
The Board repeats its 2030 recommendation, which the published statement did not take up: say 'we intend to offer a choice of engines' rather than promise it. Fitting each maker depends on its engine being ready by EIS.

