frame.asks_cfo:
Thomas, this is a consolidation round. I need your Board paper in your 'financial flexibility' register [AX-0001], on the brief's numbers only. The Board has listed what it wants, so please cover each point.

(1) PEAK CASH, YEAR BY YEAR, 2035-2039, against $5.2B (EUR 4,753m at $1.10; unrounded $5.23B):
- hold plan: $5.07B in 2035 and 2036, then nothing;
- + A350 Re-engine: $5.07B + $1.00B + $0.60B strain = $6.67B in 2035-36, then $1.00B in 2037-39;
- + one Delay Tactics move: $0.20B a year over 2035-39, so $5.27B in 2035-36;
- + both moves: about $5.47B;
- A350 Re-engine + one move: $6.87B.
Confirm or correct each, and state pass or fail.

(2) NET-CASH BRIDGE, 2035-2037, including the dividend, on the FY2025 base (net cash EUR 12.2bn [AX-0102]) and on the run-rate. The bank models stop at 2030E/2031E, and GS's DCF runs EBIT to 2036E by formula only, so label what is context. Confirm the payout guardrail: stay inside 30-50%, at the lower end if ramp cash falls short, with no equity raise, no dividend cut and no new return policy [AX-0022, AX-0104].

(3) THE 2037 CHARGE. On the board's books the single $35.52B charge lands at EIS 2037 against $18.3B of operating profit that year. Show its effect on reported earnings and on the payout base, and how we present it. Report the break-evens if any is crossed: an NGSA bill above $36.4B, FCF below EUR 4.61bn, or a rate below $1.068 per EUR. The brief still shows $35.52B.

(4) ROBUSTNESS AND DOWNSIDE. Confirm the hold plan's worst column is S2 at +22.88. Give each item column by column against hold:
- A350 Re-engine +1.54 / -1.21;
- one Delay Tactics move +1.65 / +1.66;
- both +1.18 / +1.19, the second move -0.47 / -0.47;
- cancel -39.65 / -39.65.
Confirm my arithmetic: the A350 Re-engine breaks even at a 56% chance of S2, so at my 25-30% it is worth only about +$0.7-0.85B probability-weighted.

(5) A350 RE-ENGINE TIMING. Confirm that 2045 is the first round that passes your line on these bills, and say plainly what we forgo by waiting. The 2045 value is unknown until that grid. Rule 3 applies if Boeing launches a 787 Re-engine now.

(6) INTEGRITY. As chair of the Internal Control Committee, would you raise a flag on poaching, bottleneck or both?

(7) BOARD ITEMS. Confirm that the hold plan carries none. Draft your half of a Board-gate line for any item you think could pass.

(8) Tell me plainly whether your test passes or fails for each option. For each veto, say whether it is binding and what override margin it would need: the best passing option is the hold plan.

