frame.situation:
PUBLIC RECORD, 2035 (round 1 adjudicated)
- Airframes. NGSA (Airbus) launched in 2030 and enters service in 2037. fps (Boeing) launched in 2030 on the 10-year ramp and enters service in 2040. Both chose code 7 (RR, P&W, CFM), and both will be fitted CFM + P&W + RR if nothing changes. Either can still change its code this round. Both enter service before the 2045 round, so after this round the codes lock.
- Rival narrowbody engines on the record. P&W GTF2 Solo launched in 2030 and is ready in 2036. RR UltraFan NB Solo launched in 2030 and is ready in 2037. Both are selected on both aircraft. There is no Joint Venture commitment: RR's door is open, and P&W made none. H3's trigger now sits on the public record, not just on the grid.
- Airframers' terms. Boeing: CFM is the baseline launch engine and certifies first; the others follow once they show maturity and durability. CFM must support both airframers' rates. Development is cost-plus or risk-share, never fixed price, and Boeing wants more than one source. Airbus: a choice of engines on the successor, as on the A320 family, and all three makers invited in as risk-sharing partners.
- Our programmes. The Ducted is in its last development year and is ready in 2036. Embraer counts from 2037. The GEnx upgrade has been ready since 2033 and counts from 2042, since no Re-engine is on the record.
- Observed market, 2026-34: flat. NB engines: CFM 76%, P&W 24%, RR 0%. WB engines: GEnx 42%, Trent 58%.

OUR POSITION (hold everything, S1)
- ΔPV is -62.61: engine value -56.61, R&D -4.00, strain -2.00. Yield 60.87%.
- NB share:
  - 76% through 2036;
  - 56.5% in 2037, 55.9% in 2038 and 55.2% in 2039, as NGSA splits the Airbus slot three ways;
  - 31.7% from 2040, as fps splits the 737 slot.
- WB share: 42%, then 44.8% from 2042.
- Objective, NB at least 50% in 2040, 2045 and 2050: NOT MET. The share is 31.7% in S1 and lower in S2 and S3. No row on the grid reaches 50%.
- Objective, Open Fan launched and ready by 2050: NOT MET. This is the last round in which it can still be met.

GRID READING
- Hold everything is the best plan in all three columns: S1 -62.61, S2 -89.91, S3 -78.31.
- What the code switches cost us on hold: S2 (Airbus moves NGSA to P&W only) -27.30 against S1; S3 (Boeing moves fps to RR only) -15.70. No lever on this grid defends against either. Only delivery, durability and support can, and the board does not price them.
- Every alternative loses in every column:
  - Embraer cancel: -4.65 to -6.02;
  - Ducted cancel: -16.86 to -17.65;
  - any Open Fan row: -31.37 to -59.98.
- Weighted hold: -73.36 on my preliminary weights (S1 .50, S2 .25, S3 .25); -76.94 on equal weights.

BOARD GUIDANCE (non-binding, labelled as such)
- Would approve: all five fields at `hold`, including `genx: hold`.
- Would VETO:
  - `open_fan: launch`, alone or in any combination. It waits in every column. The cheapest row, Open Fan + Lobby, is -31.37 to -37.34 against hold, over the $15B RISE allowance. There is no airframer path, and with no stated reason it is not joint practice with Safran.
  - `lobby_emissions: launch`, which does nothing without a live Open Fan.
  - `ducted: cancel`, alone or as a swap for the Open Fan. It fails the cancellation test: $3.33B written off to save $0.67B, while both airframers have selected CFM.
  - `partner_embraer: cancel`.
  - Any Board item carrying a premium. The smallest loss is $4.65B, over the $2.0B cap.
- It expects to see:
  - the expected column, with weights for all three columns;
  - the best plan against Do Nothing, in the expected column and in the worst column;
  - Ghai's tests, in numbers;
  - Ali's readiness and durability report, with any flag and its cross-functional answer, the Open Fan's 2045 shown as late, and the RISE gates reported as the state of the technology;
  - one Safran-gate line for a hold;
  - the premium counts;
  - an objective note saying plainly that both legs are missed and the Open Fan leg is out of reach on our tests;
  - no overrides;
  - a board_response to its open round-1 recommendations: the Ducted reread on the cancellation test, the RISE product call on Ali's gates, and Safran consent answered by joint practice;
  - a public statement with no rival named, no blame, no Open Fan entry year, no price or rate talk without Safran, no Ducted certification or entry claim beyond what testing has earned, and never 'next-generation' for the Ducted.

