Ask (6), a Ducted slip past 2037, refreshed.
- The float is one year: ready in 2036, counted from NGSA's 2037 entry.
- Each year lost costs $1.18B (2037), $1.11B (2038), $1.05B (2039) and $0.99B (2040), the same in all three columns. Both rival engines' moves are already in the shares, and the Ducted's +10 pp nets to about 9.5 pp after renormalisation.
- Cumulative cost to a 2040 ready year: $3.34B.
- If Embraer waits on the engine (outside the grid; inference): $1.51B (S1, S3) to $1.63B (S2) for the first year, and $4.28-4.63B to 2040.

The grid also leaves out:
- CFM's baseline, certify-first position under Boeing's terms, which a slip would put at risk;
- a Joint Venture forming in a later round (the rules take 10 pp from CFM);
- a widebody Re-engine, which brings the GEnx count earlier or puts a rival engine on the widebody;
- an airframer delivery slowdown, tariffs or a recession (the board holds NB flat at 2,000 aircraft a year).

No disagreement. My binding tests veto the same items:
- any open_fan launch;
- any lobby_emissions launch;
- a ducted cancel;
- a partner_embraer cancel;
- any item carrying a premium (the smallest is $4.65B).

One correction for the record, which does not change the verdict. The Board's 'save $0.67B' on the Ducted cancel is the R&D alone. The board row also releases the $2B strain, so the board-side saving is $2.67B, and the row still loses $16.86-17.65B.

Booking: the Board wants a figure at or below hold in the expected column, and -77 meets it.

other_moves.detail:
Ali brings the review on turn-on, turn-off evidence, not analysis [CX-0009]. It covers the gates he named for certifying first:
- the hot section turned on and turned off in hot-and-harsh dust testing [CX-0161, CX-0222];
- life-limited parts at full life at entry;
- enhanced inspection of safety-critical parts from the first engine, in new-make shops and MRO [CX-0010];
- root cause and fixes accepted by the airframer and the regulator [CX-0003].
Every design, test and production-qualification gate is a cross-functional call [CX-0012]. Any durability flag, or any slip past the one year of float to 2037, comes to the Board at once. Ghai costs a slip at $1.18B for 2037 and $3.34B cumulative to a 2040 ready year, and Embraer waiting with it adds to that. If a gate does not turn off, the date moves, not the test [CX-0208, CX-0603], and we fix the part rather than cancel the engine [CX-0005]. Until the review, 2036 is labelled the board's development time, not a test result.

RISE stays funded inside the R&D we protect first: no cancel for cost (H5) and no 'program dividend' [CX-0174, CX-0398]. Ali's report on the round-1 gates is given to the Board as the state of the technology, not as a product case:
- flight test: not shown on the record;
- compact-core durability: dust testing has started, but no turn-off result is shown;
- more than 20% fuel burn: a design and physics figure, not yet shown in engine test;
- noise below today's LEAP: shown as a capability.
In public: no RISE date, no flight-test year and no fuel-burn number.

memo_weighing.cfo:
